---
import SimLayout from '../../../layouts/SimLayout.astro';
---

<SimLayout
  areaSlug="physics"
  title="Stationary Action"
  description="Nature does not follow forces step by step, or so the other story goes: given where it starts and where it ends, it takes the path whose action cannot be improved on. Bend the path yourself and watch the number get worse, then let it fall back."
>
  <div class="sim">
    <div class="sim-stage">
      <canvas id="act" class="act-canvas" width="1000" height="880"></canvas>
    </div>

    <div class="sim-panel">
      <div style="display:flex;gap:8px;flex-wrap:wrap">
        <button class="btn" id="run">Pause</button>
        <button class="btn ghost" id="relax">Let it relax</button>
        <button class="btn ghost" id="reset">Straight line</button>
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label>What it moves in</label>
        <div class="seg" id="pot">
          <button data-p="gravity" class="active">Gravity</button>
          <button data-p="free">Nothing</button>
          <button data-p="spring">A spring</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label>Bend the path <b id="bendval">+0.0 m</b></label>
        <input type="range" id="bend" min="-100" max="100" step="1" value="45" />
        <div class="scale-hint"><span>push down</span><span>push up</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label>Where the bend is <b id="posval">middle</b></label>
        <input type="range" id="pos" min="10" max="90" step="1" value="50" />
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label>How wide the bend is <b id="widval">0.30</b></label>
        <input type="range" id="wid" min="5" max="60" step="1" value="30" />
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label>Trip time T <b id="ttval">2.0 s</b></label>
        <input type="range" id="tt" min="5" max="40" step="1" value="20" />
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label>Mass <b id="mval">1.0 kg</b></label>
        <input type="range" id="mass" min="5" max="40" step="1" value="10" />
      </div>

      <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" id="anim" checked /> Send a bead along both paths</label>

      <div class="readout">
        <div class="row"><span id="l1">Action of your path</span><b id="r1">—</b></div>
        <div class="row"><span id="l2">Action of the real path</span><b id="r2">—</b></div>
        <div class="row"><span id="l3">Your action minus theirs</span><b id="r3">—</b></div>
        <div class="row"><span id="l4">Mean Lagrangian</span><b id="r4">—</b></div>
        <div class="row"><span id="l5">Mean KE and PE</span><b id="r5">—</b></div>
        <div class="row"><span id="l6">Energy swings by</span><b id="r6">—</b></div>
        <div class="row"><span id="l7">Worst F = ma error</span><b id="r7">—</b></div>
      </div>

      <p style="font-size:.82rem;color:var(--text-faint);margin:0;line-height:1.5">
        Drag the curve in the top panel to bend it anywhere you like. The action
        is computed from your actual path, not from a formula for it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <Fragment slot="observe">
    <li>
      The two ends are pinned and everything between them is yours to choose.
      Bend the path and the action gets bigger; bend it the other way and it
      gets bigger again. The real path sits at the <b>bottom of that valley</b>,
      and that is the whole principle: not the smallest path, the one where
      small changes stop mattering.
    </li>
    <li>
      Look at the curve of S against how hard you bend. At your path the slope
      is some number; at the real path the slope is <b>zero</b>. This is what
      "stationary" means, and it is why the same idea keeps working when the
      real path is a maximum or a saddle instead of a minimum.
    </li>
    <li>
      The path is painted with its own Lagrangian: orange where the kinetic term
      is winning, blue where the potential is. Bending upward buys more time in
      the blue, which <b>subtracts less</b> from L, but you have to move faster
      to get there and back, which adds more orange. The real path is the exact
      trade between the two, and it is a parabola only because that trade
      balances where it does.
    </li>
    <li>
      In the Lagrangian panel, L is the difference of the two thin curves and
      the shaded area under it <b>is</b> the action. Bend the path and watch the
      shape of that area change: everything the principle talks about is the
      size of that patch, nothing else.
    </li>
    <li>
      Keep an eye on the dashed line for KE + PE. On the real path it is
      dead flat, because energy is conserved. On any path you bend it visibly
      wobbles, which is the sim telling you an invisible hand would have to push
      the bead to make it go that way. <b>L is not energy</b>, and the flat one
      is the sum while the interesting one is the difference.
    </li>
    <li>
      Switch to Nothing and the trade disappears: with no potential the only way
      to spend less is to not hurry, so the answer is a straight line at constant
      speed. Newton's first law falls out of a principle about a whole path.
    </li>
    <li>
      Turn on the beads and race them. Both leave and arrive together because
      both are forced to, but only one is moving the way a real object would.
      The dots along each path mark equal slices of time, so where they spread
      out the bead is fast: that spacing is the kinetic term made visible.
    </li>
    <li>
      Press "Let it relax" and watch the path slide downhill in action. Every
      step it takes is <b>ma = −dV/dx</b> being enforced a little more, because
      the gradient of the action is precisely that error. Minimising the action
      and obeying Newton are two descriptions of one thing.
    </li>
    <li>
      Try the spring and then stretch the trip time. The valley in the S curve
      gets shallower, flattens, and eventually turns into a hill: past a certain
      trip time the true path stops being the smallest action and becomes a
      saddle. Nature never promised the smallest, only that the first-order
      change vanishes, which is why the principle survives this.
    </li>
  </Fragment>
</SimLayout>

<script>
  import { setupCanvas, makeText, subHtml } from '../../../lib/sim';

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // A bead goes from A to B in a fixed time T. The action of any path is
  //   S = ∫ (½ m ẋ² − V(x)) dt
  // computed here by straight summation over the path you drew. The classical
  // path is found by rolling the same sum downhill, which is exactly Newton.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  const N = 160; // path samples
  const X0 = -1.2, X1 = 1.2; // the two ends, pinned at different heights
  const G = 9.81;

  const state = {
    pot: 'gravity' as 'gravity' | 'free' | 'spring',
    bend: 0, // metres, how far the handle pushes the path
    pos: 0.5, // where along the trip
    wid: 0.3, // how wide the bump is
    T: 2, // seconds for the whole trip
    m: 1, // kg
    k: 6, // spring constant
    anim: true,
    running: true,
  };

  /** The potential the bead moves in, and the force that goes with it. */
  const V = (x: number) => {
    if (state.pot === 'free') return 0;
    if (state.pot === 'spring') return 0.5 * state.k * x * x;
    return state.m * G * x;
  };
  const dV = (x: number) => {
    if (state.pot === 'free') return 0;
    if (state.pot === 'spring') return state.k * x;
    return state.m * G;
  };

  /** The classical path, in closed form, purely to have something to compare to. */
  function classical(f: number) {
    const t = f * state.T;
    if (state.pot === 'free') return X0 + (X1 - X0) * f;
    if (state.pot === 'spring') {
      const w = Math.sqrt(state.k / state.m);
      const s = Math.sin(w * state.T);
      if (Math.abs(s) < 1e-6) return X0 + (X1 - X0) * f; // degenerate, just draw a line
      return (X0 * Math.sin(w * (state.T - t)) + X1 * Math.sin(w * t)) / s;
    }
    // thrown straight up and falling back, with both ends pinned
    return X0 + ((X1 - X0) * t) / state.T + 0.5 * G * t * (state.T - t);
  }

  /** Your path: the classical one plus the bump you are pushing into it. */
  let bump: number[] = new Array(N + 1).fill(0);

  function baseBump(i: number) {
    const f = i / N;
    const d = (f - state.pos) / Math.max(0.02, state.wid);
    return Math.exp(-d * d * 2.2) * (1 - Math.abs(2 * f - 1) ** 8);
  }

  function pathAt(i: number, scale = 1) {
    return classical(i / N) + (bump[i] + state.bend * baseBump(i)) * scale;
  }

  /** S = Σ (½m v² − V) dt over the path exactly as drawn. */
  function action(scale = 1) {
    const dt = state.T / N;
    let s = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
      const a = pathAt(i, scale), b = pathAt(i + 1, scale);
      const v = (b - a) / dt;
      s += (0.5 * state.m * v * v - V(0.5 * (a + b))) * dt;
    }
    return s;
  }

  /** How badly the path breaks ma = −dV/dx at each interior point. */
  function residuals(scale = 1) {
    const dt = state.T / N;
    const out = new Array(N + 1).fill(0);
    for (let i = 1; i < N; i++) {
      const acc = (pathAt(i + 1, scale) - 2 * pathAt(i, scale) + pathAt(i - 1, scale)) / (dt * dt);
      out[i] = state.m * acc + dV(pathAt(i, scale));
    }
    return out;
  }

  const relaxBuf = new Array(N + 1).fill(0);

  /**
   * Steps downhill in action. δS/δx_i is exactly minus the F = ma error, so
   * rolling the path down the action is literally enforcing Newton at every
   * point.
   *
   * The step size is capped by stability at about dt²/m, which makes the
   * long-wavelength part of a bend relax only as fast as (π/N)² per step. A
   * handful of steps per frame therefore looks like nothing is happening at
   * all, hence the large count here: it is thousands of tiny steps, not a
   * different algorithm.
   */
  function relaxStep(steps: number) {
    const dt = state.T / N;
    const h = (0.4 * dt * dt) / state.m;
    let biggest = 0;
    for (let s = 0; s < steps; s++) {
      for (let i = 1; i < N; i++) {
        const acc = (pathAt(i + 1) - 2 * pathAt(i) + pathAt(i - 1)) / (dt * dt);
        relaxBuf[i] = state.m * acc + dV(pathAt(i));
      }
      biggest = 0;
      for (let i = 1; i < N; i++) {
        bump[i] += h * relaxBuf[i];
        biggest = Math.max(biggest, Math.abs(relaxBuf[i]));
      }
    }
    return biggest;
  }

  function clearBump() {
    bump = new Array(N + 1).fill(0);
  }

  // --- canvas ----------------------------------------------------------------

  const canvas = document.getElementById('act') as HTMLCanvasElement;
  const { ctx, W, H } = setupCanvas(canvas, () => draw());
  const cssVar = (n: string) => getComputedStyle(canvas).getPropertyValue(n).trim();
  const inkRGB = () => cssVar('--stage-ink') || '232,233,236';
  const inkA = (a: number) => `rgba(${inkRGB()},${a})`;
  const bg = () => cssVar('--stage-bg') || '#0d0e12';
  const { label, textWidth } = makeText(ctx, inkA);

  const YOURS = '#7c8cff';
  const REAL = '#34d399';
  const KE = '#f59e0b';
  const PE = '#5b8cff';
  const BAD = '#f9736a';

  const PATH = { x: 20, y: 86, w: 960, h: 420 };
  const SCURVE = { x: 20, y: 566, w: 470, h: 294 };
  const ENERGY = { x: 510, y: 566, w: 470, h: 294 };

  function rr(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, r: number) {
    const rad = Math.min(r, Math.abs(w) / 2, Math.abs(h) / 2);
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(x + rad, y);
    ctx.lineTo(x + w - rad, y);
    ctx.quadraticCurveTo(x + w, y, x + w, y + rad);
    ctx.lineTo(x + w, y + h - rad);
    ctx.quadraticCurveTo(x + w, y + h, x + w - rad, y + h);
    ctx.lineTo(x + rad, y + h);
    ctx.quadraticCurveTo(x, y + h, x, y + h - rad);
    ctx.lineTo(x, y + rad);
    ctx.quadraticCurveTo(x, y, x + rad, y);
    ctx.closePath();
  }
  function panel(p: { x: number; y: number; w: number; h: number }, title: string) {
    ctx.strokeStyle = inkA(0.1);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1;
    rr(p.x, p.y, p.w, p.h, 10);
    ctx.stroke();
    label(title, p.x + 2, p.y - 9, 0.5, 'left', 11);
  }

  // --- the path panel --------------------------------------------------------

  function pathBounds() {
    let lo = 0, hi = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i <= N; i++) {
      lo = Math.min(lo, pathAt(i), classical(i / N));
      hi = Math.max(hi, pathAt(i), classical(i / N));
    }
    const pad = Math.max(1.2, (hi - lo) * 0.25);
    return { lo: lo - pad, hi: hi + pad };
  }

  const plot = () => ({ x: PATH.x + 74, y: PATH.y + 40, w: PATH.w - 110, h: PATH.h - 96 });
  function mapper() {
    const p = plot(), b = pathBounds();
    return {
      X: (f: number) => p.x + f * p.w,
      Y: (x: number) => p.y + p.h - ((x - b.lo) / (b.hi - b.lo)) * p.h,
      p,
      b,
    };
  }

  function drawPath() {
    panel(PATH, 'EVERY PATH FROM A TO B, AND THE ONE NATURE TAKES');
    const { X, Y, p, b } = mapper();

    // axes
    ctx.strokeStyle = inkA(0.18);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(p.x, p.y);
    ctx.lineTo(p.x, p.y + p.h);
    ctx.lineTo(p.x + p.w, p.y + p.h);
    ctx.stroke();
    if (b.lo < 0 && b.hi > 0) {
      ctx.strokeStyle = inkA(0.12);
      ctx.setLineDash([3, 3]);
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.moveTo(p.x, Y(0));
      ctx.lineTo(p.x + p.w, Y(0));
      ctx.stroke();
      ctx.setLineDash([]);
    }
    label('height x', p.x - 10, p.y + 6, 0.4, 'right', 10);
    label('time', p.x + p.w, p.y + p.h + 18, 0.4, 'right', 10);
    label('0', p.x - 10, Y(0) + 4, 0.35, 'right', 10);
    label(`${b.hi.toFixed(1)} m`, p.x - 10, p.y + 6 + 14, 0.35, 'right', 10);
    label('A', p.x - 16, Y(X0) + 5, 0.8, 'right', 12);
    label('B', p.x + p.w + 12, Y(X1) + 5, 0.8, 'left', 12);
    label(`T = ${state.T.toFixed(1)} s`, p.x + p.w / 2, p.y + p.h + 18, 0.4, 'center', 10);

    // where your path is breaking Newton, drawn as a band under it
    const res = residuals();
    let rmax = 1e-9;
    for (const r of res) rmax = Math.max(rmax, Math.abs(r));
    for (let i = 1; i < N; i++) {
      const a = Math.min(1, Math.abs(res[i]) / rmax);
      if (a < 0.04) continue;
      ctx.strokeStyle = `rgba(249,115,106,${a * 0.55})`;
      ctx.lineWidth = 2 + a * 12;
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.moveTo(X(i / N), Y(pathAt(i)));
      ctx.lineTo(X((i + 1) / N), Y(pathAt(i + 1)));
      ctx.stroke();
    }

    // the classical path
    ctx.strokeStyle = REAL;
    ctx.setLineDash([6, 5]);
    ctx.lineWidth = 2;
    ctx.beginPath();
    for (let i = 0; i <= N; i++) {
      const yy = Y(classical(i / N));
      i ? ctx.lineTo(X(i / N), yy) : ctx.moveTo(X(i / N), yy);
    }
    ctx.stroke();
    ctx.setLineDash([]);

    // Your path, tinted by the Lagrangian at each moment: orange where the
    // kinetic term wins, blue where the potential does. The action is the
    // running balance of exactly this colour.
    const { L } = series();
    let lmax = 1e-9;
    for (const v of L) lmax = Math.max(lmax, Math.abs(v));
    ctx.lineWidth = 4;
    for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
      const t = Math.max(-1, Math.min(1, L[i] / lmax));
      const mix = (t + 1) / 2; // 0 = potential wins, 1 = kinetic wins
      const r = Math.round(91 + mix * (245 - 91));
      const g = Math.round(140 + mix * (158 - 140));
      const b = Math.round(255 + mix * (11 - 255));
      ctx.strokeStyle = `rgb(${r},${g},${b})`;
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.moveTo(X(i / N), Y(pathAt(i)));
      ctx.lineTo(X((i + 1) / N), Y(pathAt(i + 1)));
      ctx.stroke();
    }

    // Ticks at equal time steps: where they bunch the bead is slow, where they
    // spread it is fast, so the kinetic term is visible on the path itself.
    const nTick = 24;
    for (let k = 0; k <= nTick; k++) {
      const f = k / nTick;
      const i = Math.round(f * N);
      ctx.fillStyle = inkA(0.5);
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.arc(X(f), Y(pathAt(i)), 2.4, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      ctx.fill();
      ctx.fillStyle = 'rgba(52,211,153,0.5)';
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.arc(X(f), Y(classical(f)), 2.4, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      ctx.fill();
    }

    // the pinned ends
    for (const [f, x] of [[0, X0], [1, X1]] as [number, number][]) {
      ctx.fillStyle = inkA(0.8);
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.arc(X(f), Y(x), 6, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      ctx.fill();
    }

    // beads running both paths at once
    if (state.anim) {
      const f = (clock * 0.42) % 1.25;
      if (f <= 1) {
        const i = Math.max(0, Math.min(N, Math.round(f * N)));
        ctx.fillStyle = YOURS;
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.arc(X(f), Y(pathAt(i)), 8, 0, Math.PI * 2);
        ctx.fill();
        ctx.fillStyle = REAL;
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.arc(X(f), Y(classical(f)), 8, 0, Math.PI * 2);
        ctx.fill();
      }
    }

    label('your path, tinted by L', p.x + 8, p.y + 18, 0.85, 'left', 12);
    label('orange: kinetic wins, L > 0', p.x + 8, p.y + 36, 0.7, 'left', 11, KE);
    label('blue: potential wins, L < 0', p.x + 8, p.y + 54, 0.7, 'left', 11, PE);
    label('dashed green: what really happens', p.x + 8, p.y + 72, 0.7, 'left', 11, REAL);
    label('red halo: where F = ma is broken', p.x + 8, p.y + 90, 0.7, 'left', 11, BAD);
    label('drag the curve to bend it', p.x + p.w, p.y + 18, 0.45, 'right', 11);
    label('dots are equal time steps: wide apart means fast', p.x + p.w, p.y + 36, 0.4, 'right', 10);
  }

  // --- the action landscape --------------------------------------------------

  /**
   * The direction the bend pushes the path in, scaled so its biggest
   * displacement is one metre, together with how far you have actually pushed
   * it. Splitting shape from size is what lets the plot below be a fixed
   * landscape that your marker slides along, instead of a curve that silently
   * renormalises itself every time you move the slider.
   */
  function deformation() {
    let peak = 0, at = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i <= N; i++) {
      const d = bump[i] + state.bend * baseBump(i);
      if (Math.abs(d) > Math.abs(peak)) {
        peak = d;
        at = i;
      }
    }
    if (Math.abs(peak) < 1e-4) {
      // Nothing of your own to push along, so use the handle's own shape and
      // sit at zero. Falling back keeps the picture continuous.
      return { dir: (i: number) => baseBump(i), amp: 0, at: Math.round(state.pos * N) };
    }
    const scale = 1 / peak;
    return { dir: (i: number) => (bump[i] + state.bend * baseBump(i)) * scale, amp: peak, at };
  }

  /** S of the classical path displaced by `x` metres along that direction. */
  function actionAlong(dir: (i: number) => number, x: number) {
    const dt = state.T / N;
    let s = 0;
    let prev = classical(0) + dir(0) * x;
    for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
      const next = classical((i + 1) / N) + dir(i + 1) * x;
      const v = (next - prev) / dt;
      s += (0.5 * state.m * v * v - V(0.5 * (prev + next))) * dt;
      prev = next;
    }
    return s;
  }

  function drawSCurve() {
    panel(SCURVE, 'THE ACTION AS THE PATH IS PUSHED ASIDE');
    const px = SCURVE.x + 74, py = SCURVE.y + 30, pw = SCURVE.w - 100, ph = SCURVE.h - 88;
    const { dir, amp } = deformation();
    // A fixed window in metres, widened only if you have pushed past it, so the
    // curve stays put and the marker is the thing that moves.
    const A = Math.max(5.2, Math.abs(amp) * 1.15);
    const K = 72;
    const vals: number[] = [];
    for (let k = 0; k <= K; k++) vals.push(actionAlong(dir, -A + (2 * A * k) / K));
    const lo = Math.min(...vals), hi = Math.max(...vals);
    const span = Math.max(1e-6, hi - lo);
    const X = (x: number) => px + ((x + A) / (2 * A)) * pw;
    const Y = (sv: number) => py + ph - ((sv - lo) / span) * ph;

    ctx.strokeStyle = inkA(0.18);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(px, py);
    ctx.lineTo(px, py + ph);
    ctx.lineTo(px + pw, py + ph);
    ctx.stroke();
    for (const m of [-4, -2, 2, 4]) {
      if (Math.abs(m) > A) continue;
      ctx.strokeStyle = inkA(0.07);
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.moveTo(X(m), py);
      ctx.lineTo(X(m), py + ph);
      ctx.stroke();
      label(`${m}`, X(m), py + ph + 16, 0.3, 'center', 9);
    }

    ctx.strokeStyle = YOURS;
    ctx.lineWidth = 2.4;
    ctx.beginPath();
    vals.forEach((sv, k) => {
      const x = -A + (2 * A * k) / K;
      k ? ctx.lineTo(X(x), Y(sv)) : ctx.moveTo(X(x), Y(sv));
    });
    ctx.stroke();

    // zero push is the real path, and the tangent there is the whole point
    const s0 = actionAlong(dir, 0);
    const eps = 0.01;
    const slope = (actionAlong(dir, eps) - actionAlong(dir, -eps)) / (2 * eps);
    const curv = (actionAlong(dir, eps) - 2 * s0 + actionAlong(dir, -eps)) / (eps * eps);
    ctx.strokeStyle = REAL;
    ctx.setLineDash([4, 4]);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1.3;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(X(0), py);
    ctx.lineTo(X(0), py + ph);
    ctx.stroke();
    ctx.setLineDash([]);
    ctx.strokeStyle = 'rgba(52,211,153,0.75)';
    ctx.lineWidth = 1.6;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(X(-1.6), Y(s0 - slope * 1.6));
    ctx.lineTo(X(1.6), Y(s0 + slope * 1.6));
    ctx.stroke();
    ctx.fillStyle = REAL;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.arc(X(0), Y(s0), 5.5, 0, Math.PI * 2);
    ctx.fill();
    label('the real path', X(0) + 12, Y(s0) + (curv > 0 ? -14 : 20), 0.85, 'left', 11, REAL);

    // where you are, which slides along the curve as you push
    const sYou = actionAlong(dir, amp);
    ctx.fillStyle = YOURS;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.arc(X(amp), Y(sYou), 6, 0, Math.PI * 2);
    ctx.fill();
    ctx.strokeStyle = bg();
    ctx.lineWidth = 1.5;
    ctx.stroke();
    label(
      `you, ${amp >= 0 ? '+' : ''}${amp.toFixed(2)} m`,
      X(amp),
      Y(sYou) + (curv > 0 ? -14 : 20),
      0.9,
      'center',
      11,
      YOURS
    );

    label('how far the path is pushed, in metres', px + pw / 2, py + ph + 32, 0.4, 'center', 10);
    label('S', px - 10, py + 8, 0.4, 'right', 10);
    label(`span ${(hi - lo).toFixed(2)} J s`, px + pw, py + 12, 0.35, 'right', 9);
    label(
      curv > 0
        ? 'flat tangent: push either way and S goes up'
        : 'flat, but a maximum: stationary is not always smallest',
      SCURVE.x + 14,
      SCURVE.y + SCURVE.h - 14,
      0.5,
      'left',
      11,
      curv > 0 ? REAL : KE
    );
  }

  // --- the Lagrangian itself -------------------------------------------------

  /** Kinetic, potential, their difference and their sum, sample by sample. */
  function series(scale = 1) {
    const dt = state.T / N;
    const ke: number[] = [], pe: number[] = [], L: number[] = [], E: number[] = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
      const a = pathAt(i, scale), b = pathAt(i + 1, scale);
      const v = (b - a) / dt;
      const k = 0.5 * state.m * v * v;
      const u = V(0.5 * (a + b));
      ke.push(k);
      pe.push(u);
      L.push(k - u);
      E.push(k + u);
    }
    return { ke, pe, L, E };
  }

  function drawLagrangian() {
    panel(ENERGY, 'THE LAGRANGIAN L = KE − PE, AND THE AREA UNDER IT');
    const px = ENERGY.x + 66, py = ENERGY.y + 34, pw = ENERGY.w - 92, ph = ENERGY.h - 104;
    const { ke, pe, L, E } = series();
    let top = 1e-9;
    for (let i = 0; i < N; i++)
      top = Math.max(top, Math.abs(ke[i]), Math.abs(pe[i]), Math.abs(L[i]), Math.abs(E[i]));
    const X = (i: number) => px + (i / (N - 1)) * pw;
    const Y = (e: number) => py + ph / 2 - (e / (top * 1.1)) * (ph / 2);

    // the area under L is the action, so draw it as an area
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(px, Y(0));
    L.forEach((e, i) => ctx.lineTo(X(i), Y(e)));
    ctx.lineTo(X(N - 1), Y(0));
    ctx.closePath();
    ctx.fillStyle = 'rgba(124,140,255,0.22)';
    ctx.fill();

    ctx.strokeStyle = inkA(0.2);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(px, py);
    ctx.lineTo(px, py + ph);
    ctx.moveTo(px, Y(0));
    ctx.lineTo(px + pw, Y(0));
    ctx.stroke();

    const line = (arr: number[], col: string, lw: number, dash: number[] = []) => {
      ctx.strokeStyle = col;
      ctx.lineWidth = lw;
      ctx.setLineDash(dash);
      ctx.beginPath();
      arr.forEach((e, i) => (i ? ctx.lineTo(X(i), Y(e)) : ctx.moveTo(X(i), Y(e))));
      ctx.stroke();
      ctx.setLineDash([]);
    };
    line(ke, KE, 1.4);
    line(pe, PE, 1.4);
    line(E, inkA(0.45), 1.4, [5, 4]);
    line(L, YOURS, 2.6);

    const mean = (a: number[]) => a.reduce((t, x) => t + x, 0) / a.length;
    const wobble = Math.max(...E) - Math.min(...E);

    label('KE', px + pw + 6, Y(ke[N - 1]) + 4, 0.85, 'left', 10, KE);
    label('PE', px + pw + 6, Y(pe[N - 1]) + 4, 0.85, 'left', 10, PE);
    label('L', px + pw + 6, Y(L[N - 1]) + 4, 0.9, 'left', 11, YOURS);
    label('E', px + pw + 6, Y(E[N - 1]) + 4, 0.6, 'left', 10);
    label('0', px - 8, Y(0) + 4, 0.35, 'right', 10);
    label('time', px + pw, py + ph + 16, 0.4, 'right', 10);
    label('shaded area = the action', px + 6, py + 14, 0.7, 'left', 11, YOURS);

    label(
      `S = mean L × T = ${mean(L).toFixed(2)} × ${state.T.toFixed(1)} = ${(mean(L) * state.T).toFixed(2)} J s`,
      ENERGY.x + 14,
      ENERGY.y + ENERGY.h - 36,
      0.6,
      'left',
      11
    );
    label(
      wobble < 0.05
        ? 'KE + PE flat: only the real path conserves energy'
        : `KE + PE swings ${wobble.toFixed(1)} J: something is pushing this`,
      ENERGY.x + 14,
      ENERGY.y + ENERGY.h - 16,
      0.5,
      'left',
      11,
      wobble < 0.05 ? REAL : BAD
    );
  }

  // --- readouts --------------------------------------------------------------

  const el = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id) as HTMLElement;
  const setRow = (n: number, lab: string, val: string) => {
    el('l' + n).innerHTML = subHtml(lab);
    el('r' + n).textContent = val;
  };
  function updateReadout() {
    const s1 = action(1), s0 = action(0);
    const { ke, pe, L, E } = series();
    const mean = (a: number[]) => a.reduce((t, x) => t + x, 0) / a.length;
    const res = residuals();
    let rmax = 0;
    for (const r of res) rmax = Math.max(rmax, Math.abs(r));
    setRow(1, 'Action of your path', s1.toFixed(3) + ' J s');
    setRow(2, 'Action of the real path', s0.toFixed(3) + ' J s');
    setRow(3, 'Your action minus theirs', (s1 - s0).toFixed(4) + ' J s');
    setRow(4, 'Mean Lagrangian', mean(L).toFixed(3) + ' J');
    setRow(5, 'Mean KE and PE', `${mean(ke).toFixed(2)} / ${mean(pe).toFixed(2)} J`);
    setRow(6, 'Energy KE + PE swings by', (Math.max(...E) - Math.min(...E)).toFixed(3) + ' J');
    setRow(7, 'Worst F = ma error', rmax.toFixed(3) + ' N');
  }

  // --- loop ------------------------------------------------------------------

  let clock = 0;
  let relaxing = false;

  function draw() {
    ctx.clearRect(0, 0, W, H);
    label('Stationary Action', 20, 30, 0.85, 'left', 14);
    const s1 = action(1), s0 = action(0);
    // The difference can go either way: past a conjugate point the real path is
    // a maximum, and saying "costs more" there would be a lie.
    const diff = s1 - s0;
    label(
      Math.abs(diff) < 1e-3
        ? 'you are on the real path: to first order nothing you do to it changes S'
        : diff > 0
          ? `your path costs ${diff.toFixed(3)} J s more action than the real one`
          : `your path has ${(-diff).toFixed(3)} J s less action: here the real path is a maximum`,
      20,
      52,
      0.5,
      'left',
      11,
      Math.abs(diff) < 1e-3 ? REAL : YOURS
    );
    const legend: [string, string][] = [
      [KE, 'kinetic wins'],
      [PE, 'potential wins'],
      [REAL, 'the real path'],
    ];
    let x = W - 20;
    for (let k = legend.length - 1; k >= 0; k--) {
      const [c, tx] = legend[k];
      const tw = textWidth(tx, 11);
      label(tx, x, 30, 0.55, 'right', 11);
      ctx.fillStyle = c;
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.arc(x - tw - 11, 26, 5, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      ctx.fill();
      x -= tw + 30;
    }
    if (relaxing) label('relaxing: rolling the path downhill in S', W - 20, 52, 0.7, 'right', 11, KE);
    drawPath();
    drawSCurve();
    drawLagrangian();
  }

  let last = performance.now();
  function frame(now: number) {
    const dt = Math.min(0.05, Math.max(0, (now - last) / 1000));
    last = now;
    if (state.running) clock += dt;
    if (relaxing) {
      const worst = relaxStep(600);
      if (worst < 1e-3) relaxing = false;
    }
    draw();
    updateReadout();
    requestAnimationFrame(frame);
  }

  // --- controls --------------------------------------------------------------

  const runBtn = el('run');
  runBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
    state.running = !state.running;
    runBtn.textContent = state.running ? 'Pause' : 'Run';
  });
  el('reset').addEventListener('click', () => {
    clearBump();
    relaxing = false;
    const inp = el('bend') as HTMLInputElement;
    inp.value = '0';
    state.bend = 0;
    el('bendval').textContent = '+0.0 m';
  });
  el('relax').addEventListener('click', () => {
    // fold the handle into the path so relaxing has something to work on
    for (let i = 0; i <= N; i++) bump[i] += state.bend * baseBump(i);
    const inp = el('bend') as HTMLInputElement;
    inp.value = '0';
    state.bend = 0;
    el('bendval').textContent = '+0.0 m';
    relaxing = true;
  });

  function bindRange(id: string, apply: (n: number) => void) {
    const inp = el(id) as HTMLInputElement;
    const run = () => apply(+inp.value);
    inp.addEventListener('input', run);
    run();
  }
  bindRange('bend', (n) => {
    state.bend = n / 20;
    el('bendval').textContent = `${state.bend >= 0 ? '+' : ''}${state.bend.toFixed(1)} m`;
  });
  bindRange('pos', (n) => {
    state.pos = n / 100;
    el('posval').textContent = n === 50 ? 'middle' : `${n}% along`;
  });
  bindRange('wid', (n) => {
    state.wid = n / 100;
    el('widval').textContent = state.wid.toFixed(2);
  });
  bindRange('tt', (n) => {
    state.T = n / 10;
    el('ttval').textContent = `${state.T.toFixed(1)} s`;
  });
  bindRange('mass', (n) => {
    state.m = n / 10;
    el('mval').textContent = `${state.m.toFixed(1)} kg`;
  });
  (el('anim') as HTMLInputElement).addEventListener('change', (e) => {
    state.anim = (e.target as HTMLInputElement).checked;
  });
  el('pot').addEventListener('click', (e) => {
    const btn = (e.target as HTMLElement).closest('button');
    if (!btn) return;
    document.querySelectorAll('#pot button').forEach((b) => b.classList.remove('active'));
    btn.classList.add('active');
    state.pot = btn.dataset.p as 'gravity' | 'free' | 'spring';
    clearBump();
  });

  // dragging the curve itself: the most direct way to ask "what if it went here"
  let dragging = false;
  function drag(e: PointerEvent) {
    const rect = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
    const mx = ((e.clientX - rect.left) / rect.width) * W;
    const my = ((e.clientY - rect.top) / rect.height) * H;
    const { p, b } = mapper();
    if (mx < p.x || mx > p.x + p.w) return;
    const f = (mx - p.x) / p.w;
    const xWanted = b.lo + ((p.y + p.h - my) / p.h) * (b.hi - b.lo);
    const i = Math.round(f * N);
    const delta = xWanted - pathAt(i);
    // spread the pull over a smooth patch so the path stays a path
    const wpx = Math.max(0.05, state.wid * 0.5);
    for (let k = 1; k < N; k++) {
      const d = (k / N - f) / wpx;
      bump[k] += delta * Math.exp(-d * d * 2) * 0.5;
    }
    relaxing = false;
  }
  canvas.addEventListener('pointerdown', (e) => {
    const rect = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
    const my = ((e.clientY - rect.top) / rect.height) * H;
    if (my < PATH.y || my > PATH.y + PATH.h) return;
    dragging = true;
    canvas.setPointerCapture(e.pointerId);
    drag(e);
  });
  canvas.addEventListener('pointermove', (e) => {
    if (dragging) drag(e);
  });
  const stop = () => (dragging = false);
  canvas.addEventListener('pointerup', stop);
  canvas.addEventListener('pointercancel', stop);

  requestAnimationFrame(frame);
</script>

<style>
  .act-canvas {
    cursor: grab;
    touch-action: none;
  }
  .act-canvas:active {
    cursor: grabbing;
  }
  .scale-hint {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    color: var(--text-faint);
    margin-top: 3px;
  }
  .check {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
  .check input {
    accent-color: var(--accent);
  }
</style>
